---
title: How to structure a Terraform project
date: 2026-08-07
excerpt: A module layering convention — blueprint, functional, base, base-blueprint — for keeping a Terraform codebase DRY, understandable, and free of circular dependencies.
tags: [Terraform, IaC]
---

Related: [How to create a Terraform module](/blog/how-to-create-a-terraform-module).

## Our goals

### Avoid circular dependencies

This is the worst thing that can happen in an infrastructure project — it means you can't deploy your infrastructure from scratch.

![Circular dependencies](/blog/how-to-structure-a-terraform-project/structure-tf-project.png)

### Avoid drift

Drift is the term for when the real-world state of your infrastructure differs from the state defined in your configuration. This can happen for many reasons; within the context of your configuration, it happens when adding or removing resources, or changing resource definitions.

### Architecture DRY

> DRY = Don't Repeat Yourself

This means you don't have to duplicate code to create resources.

### Understandable

We want a Terraform codebase to be understandable, in order to onboard anyone on it easily and make navigating the infrastructure easier.

That means anyone, even with limited infrastructure knowledge, can understand what's created where.

### Reusable

Since we don't want to create a fully new, complete structure for each project, the project-specific part has to be as minimal as possible.

## Modules

> Before going over the module structure, some definitions are required.

### Kinds

| Name           | Purpose                                                 |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| blueprint      | Define the dependencies                                 |
| functional     | Define the business logic                               |
| base           | Define a standard structure on one/multiple resource(s) |
| base-blueprint | Define a standard complex block of multiple base modules |

### Blueprint

The `blueprint` module maps the different parts of your infrastructure and clearly expresses the dependencies between them.

It cannot contain any business logic, except global variables about the environment.

More information on that in the section below.

### Functional

A `functional` module implements multiple resources of your infrastructure. For example, say you have to implement a SQL Server and a Cosmos DB — those resources would be created under a `functional` module: `storage`.

This kind of module exposes, through locals, the configuration of created resources in a readable way.

To create those resources, the `functional` module calls several `base` and `base-blueprint` modules.

### Base

A `base` module is a pass-through from the Terraform provider documentation. It must implement all available arguments and output the resource itself.

The naming convention for a `base` module is: `${PROVIDER}-${RESOURCE_TYPE}`.

> Worth being honest about the tradeoff here: wrapping every single resource type in its own pass-through module is a style a fair amount of the community has since moved away from — it's an indirection layer that has to be kept in lockstep with every new argument a provider ships, for limited benefit over calling the resource directly from a `functional` module. It buys you a single, consistent place to enforce conventions across every use of a resource type, which is real value at scale — but on a smaller codebase, it's fair to skip the `base` layer and call resources directly.

### Base Blueprint

A `base-blueprint` module is a complex module introducing logic between `base` modules.

For example, a `base-blueprint` module called `azurerm-function-apps-blueprint` would implement the following `base` modules:

- `azurerm-function-apps`
- `azurerm-storage-account`
- `azurerm-storage-container`
- `azurerm-application-insights`

And would output all of those resources.

The naming convention for a `base-blueprint` module is: `${PROVIDER}-${RESOURCE_TYPE}-blueprint`.

## Folder structure

```bash
./
├── environment_x/
│  ├── main.tf
│  ├── outputs.tf
│  ├── providers.tf
│  └── versions.tf
│
├── environment_y/
│  ├── main.tf
│  ├── outputs.tf
│  ├── providers.tf
│  └── versions.tf
│
├── modules/
│  ├── blueprint/
│  ├── base/
│  └── functional/
│
├── .gitignore
├── .pre-commit-config.yaml
├── .tfswitchrc
├── GUIDELINES.md
└── README.md
```

### Root files

| Filename                | Explanation                                                               |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| .gitignore              | Generated by [gitignore.io](https://www.toptal.com/developers/gitignore) |
| .pre-commit-config.yaml | Runs local checks before pushing to the repository                       |
| .tfswitchrc              | Makes sure you're running the correct Terraform version                  |
| GUIDELINES.md           | Explains the rules to contribute to and develop on this codebase         |
| README.md               | Explains the purpose of your infrastructure, includes architecture diagrams |

### Environments folder

To create an environment, the only required parameter is the name of the environment.

`versions.tf` and `providers.tf` describe and configure the providers required for your implementation.

`outputs.tf` gives you architecture information if needed.

`main.tf` calls the `blueprint` module.

```hcl
module "blueprint" {
  source = "../modules/blueprints"

  name = "environment_x"
}
```

### Blueprint module

This module represents the dependencies between the functional modules.

It also defines the global variables and the context.

```hcl
# Create or retrieve your resource group
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "this" {
  name     = "RG-${var.name}"
  location = "northeurope"
}

# Define the global variables
locals {
  code = {
    "environment_x" = "X",
    "environment_y" = "Y",
  }

  context = {
    project_name     = "xxx"
    environment      = var.name
    environment_code = local.code[var.name]
    resource_group   = azurerm_resource_group.this
  }
}

# Call your functional modules and inject inner dependencies
module "storage" {
  source  = "../functional/storage"
  context = local.context
}

module "compute" {
  source  = "../functional/compute"
  context = local.context
  storage = module.storage.sql
}

...
```

### Functional modules

This kind of module implements the business logic for your infrastructure. It declares, through locals, all the different parts of your infrastructure in a human-readable way.

```hcl
locals {
  storage_accounts = {
    "foo" = {
      replication = "GRS"
    },
    "bar" = {
      replication = "LRS"
    },
  }
}

module "storage_accounts" {
  for_each = local.storage_accounts
  source   = "../base/azurerm-storage-accounts"

  name        = each.key
  replication = each.value["replication"]
}
```

## Alternatives

There are a lot of technologies that can help you create a structure for a Terraform project, such as:

- [Terragrunt — Terraform wrapper](https://terragrunt.gruntwork.io/)
- [Terraspace — The Terraform Framework](https://terraspace.cloud/)
